my name is rin harasaki and i've played  more than 2 800 hours of breath of the  wild including 60 plus videos which  counted to more than 30 million views  it's safe to say that i play this game a  little too much but when it comes to  open world adventure games breath of the  wild is obviously not the only contender  how does horizon forbidden west gorilla  game's nose addition to the horizon  series fair against breath of the wild  while these two are vastly different  games they share a lot of similarities  and some interesting design choices that  caught my attention while playing so if  you enjoy these types of videos  subscribe to gamespot to keep up with  the latest breath of the wild content  first let's start with specific points  that caught my attention about  exploration and the open world for me  personally breath of the wild has made  it really hard to judge other open world  games namely for the immersiveness and  rules of the world breath of the wild  absolutely dominated with from metallic  items attracting lightning wind  affecting loose objects on the ground or  stasising a chopped tree over a bundle  of wood to make a makeshift roof for  your campfire it's just a drop in the  bucket for the amount of things you can  do breath of the wild aspects some games  here and there have implemented in  chunks but never as well as games like  breath of the wild or red dead  redemption 2 so there's just no way to  compare on that front to horizon  vegetation doesn't burn trees can't be  cut and weather has very little gameplay  effects for aeloy so no other world for  me is felt as immersive that said what  it lacks complexity makes up for in a  world filled with things to do that are  well designed and incorporated with  camps to take out caves to explore  enemies to defeat and farm parts people  to help and arenas to fight in are just  a few of the many activities that will  keep you going in the forbidden west  verticality and climbing and horizon has  been tough to judge they definitely  improved on the sequel's climbing  mechanics with interesting use of  dynamic handholds that lets alloy move  across rocky sides in more than one way  the combinations of the way foot and  hand holds change naturally for ailoy as  she climbs is really well done but this  mechanic for some reason is inconsistent  across the world and this is what makes  it weaker many random cliffsides that  are just as rocky are not climable for  no obvious reason in breath of the wild  basically everything is climbable with  the only exceptions being shrines and  puzzles to prevent link from climbing  where it was necessary for how the area  was designed to complete the puzzle  which makes sense while unfortunate it's  not a complete deal breaker with horizon  being a much more whole experience with  not only the standard walk climb glide  and ride amount option but underwater  freedom as well and another amazing  option i'll talk about later in the  video the arrival of horizon forbidden  west's shield wing will give players an  instant comparison to the paraglider  upon playing the game though they will  find out that the shield wing is fairly  weak compared to breath of the wild  handy glider while a can cross chasms in  certain dangerous places it drops in  gravity a little too quickly and more  time than not is more to prevent falling  to your depth rather than for long  distance travel i feel like this may be  a balancing choice though as the  shielding has no stamina system and can  be used without worrying about a ticking  timer and mostly the point of gliding is  to give players a reward of traveling  distances and downhill easily after an  arduous climb which the shield wing does  that being said while it's not as good  as breath of the wild glider  specifically its newest addition to the  horizon series was very much needed to  traverse the world more freely and i  think it's hands down a way cooler  looking concept compared to breath of  the wild's simple cloth on branches  paraglider the ubisoft's towers return  in the same fashion as it did in the  first horizon with interesting puzzles  to solve to try to climb the towering  beast called talnex the tallnecks are  still one of the most interesting tower  designs i've seen basically a living  tower that reveals a portion of the map  and every tallneck poses different  challenges just like breath of the  wild's varied challenges with its chica  towers as well both were interesting  concepts in figuring out how to climb  the tower but breath of the wild takes  over in a tower's value after unlocking  it as teleporting to them in horizon  doesn't let you on the tallneck for some  reason breath of the wild's towers are  designed as an integral part of the  gameplay giving you height and  surveillance to automatically let you  get a nice aerial boost to your next  objective  side quests and interactions and horizon  for bed and west characters have been a  genuine pleasure even with a couple bugs  i've never been more invested in  characters of a world than in forbidden  west because everyone is scanned or  motion captured it's hard to compare to  breath of the wild in this respect  because breath of the wild has just the  tried and true simple text boxes and  animated characters and facial motion  capture for a zelda game would just be  plain weird but with the advances in  technology and guerrillas massive amount  of work put into this aspect it pays off  hard in my opinion and leaves me  listening to more conversations than i  ever did in breath of the wild or even  other games when it comes to combat i'm  personally a connoisseur of finding  advanced tricks with horizon's melee  combat can get quite intricate with some  things i found were instant shield wing  cancels to quickly cancel her jump off  attack and half moon cancels to cancel  out of any grounded animation  even letting her dodge roll twice in a  row with no end leg unfortunately melee  combat does such petty damage compared  to ranged equipment halo uses so melee  combat uses are way more niche compared  to breath of the wild advanced combat  techniques which can really give you the  upper hand during combat that's not  really important to the average player  though how does aeloy's fighting style  compare with breath of the wild combat  while they both have melee attacks bow  and arrow ranged attacks and  concentration slowdown they're really  nothing alike aloy is a blast to control  and fighting machines requires a  completely different approach to breath  of the wild's enemies you can gain the  upper hand quickly by tearing or  destroying machine armor and parts and  each piece that comes off has an effect  on how they act and can attack after  playing for a while it's very clear that  this is a centerpiece gorilla has  focused on in the horizon series of  picking and choosing pieces of enemies  to break off for strategizing your  approach breath of the wild has us in a  very simple method of just disarming the  enemy burning shields and leg plates off  enemies or cutting off a guardian's legs  horizon has again iterated on the  structure with new enemies and more  options and it feels great to strategize  by taking off certain parts first such  as a tremor tusk's extremely annoying  side salvo cannons all in all the flow  and style of combat from both games is  extremely different and horizon has its  own personal flavor i enjoy quite a bit  the skill tree has depth the valor surge  is extremely satisfying to pull off the  weapon techniques are powerful and the  whole system works extremely well along  with the overriding system and stealth  gameplay the core gameplay loop is  something gorilla games should be proud  of  comparing graphics is in this case  apples to oranges what i will say is  that horizon is an absolutely stunning  looking game and both performance and  resolution modes are breathtaking and  just shows guerilla's strong arm in the  visual fidelity department they broke  conventions during their kill zone years  which i played plenty of kills on too  and they continue to push the boundaries  of stunning visuals to this day breath  of the wild on the other hand on a  five-year-old switch which even back on  launch wasn't nearly as powerful as a  ps4 xbox one is not comparable in this  regard but it doesn't need to be as  nintendo designed this art style to  match the graphical capabilities of the  switch and it works well for the land of  hyrule the semi-cel-shaded look of  breath of the wild and the high fidelity  west coast of the forbidden west are  both beautiful in their own ways  now for anyone who hasn't gone too far  in horizon forbidden west there's one  last thing that i admired the most but  note that this is an equipment spoiler  but doesn't spoil the story so if you  want to skip this skip to the next time  stamp now probably my favorite part of  the game comes after you pick up the  flying sun wing mount sun wings give you  a massive amount of exploration freedom  and makes traversing the landscape  effortless a perfect near end game  reward this in a sense is a much more  scaled up and realized version of  skyward swords loftwing and i absolutely  love it this kind of mount would have  been amazing for breath of the wild as  well although in my opinion would have  to have been nerfed in some capacity as  it would have made exploration too  trivial in hyrule we already have some  form of this in a fairly new glitch  called blss which we have a video about  explaining here but as expected for a  normal player it completely trivializes  casual exploration and breath of the  wild  the way the sun wing effortlessly picks  you up out of the air mid-flight or  scoops you up out of open waters the way  it circles around you during combat so  it can give you a chance to zip up out  of the fight at any time the satisfying  dive bomb effects and even the ability  to land and walk on the ground which  isn't all that useful but gives the  sunwing much more character as a  friendly mount is also well designed  this was probably my favorite part of  horizon forbidden west and while there  are flying mounts in other games i'd  like to see other games run with this  design even farther  overall horizon forbidden west was a  blast to play what i also will say is  that while horizon could have added more  aspects like breath of the wild it's  obviously its own game and adding some  of my suggestions would have added  unnecessary fluff it's clear gorilla  games had a specific goal in mind during  development and focused on what made  horizon specifically strong still  sometimes i forget that horizon zero  dawn was released before breath of the  wild and it's cool to see gorilla games  took some notes from breath of the  wild's design and its next game if you  want to keep up with the latest horizon  forbidden west or breath of the wild  content keep it here on gamespot  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

